 risky sexual behavior among African American women</article-title><source>Women Health</source><year>1998</year><volume>27</volume><fpage>17</fpage><lpage>35</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1300/J013v27n03_02</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B27"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Loue</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><source>Gender, Ethnicity, and Health Research</source><year>1999</year><publisher-name>New York: Kluwer Academic/Plenum</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lamensdorf Ofori-Atta</surname><given-names>AML</given-names></name><name><surname>Linden</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The effect of social change on causal belief of mental disorders and treatment preferences in Ghana</article-title><source>Soc Sc Med</source><year>1995</year><volume>40</volume><fpage>1231</fpage><lpage>1242</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0277-9536(94)00248-R</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B29"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Berry</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Brislin R</surname></name></person-group><article-title>Psychology of acculturation: Understanding individuals moving between cultures</article-title><source>Applied Cross-Cultural Psychology</source><year>1990</year><publisher-name>Newbury Park: Sage</publisher-name><fpage>232</fpage><lpage>253</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Reece</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Palmgreen</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Coming to America: Need for acculturation and media use motives among Indian sojourners in the US</article-title><source>Int J Intercult Relat</source><year>2000</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>802</fpage><lpage>824</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0147-1767(00)00033-X</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B31"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brannen</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><collab>ed</collab></person-group><source>Mixing methods: Qualitative and quantitative research</source><year>1992</year><publisher-name>London: Avebury</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B32"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Neuman</surname><given-names>WL</given-names></name></person-group><source>Social Research Methods: Qualitative and quantitative approaches</source><year>2000</year><edition>4</edition><publisher-name>London: Allyn &#x00026; Bacon</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B33"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Corbetta</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><source>Social Research: Theory, Methods and Techniques</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>London: Sage</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B34"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Forrest</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Van Teijlingen</surname><given-names>ER</given-names></name><name><surname>Pitchforth</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The analysis of qualitative research data in family planning and reproductive health care</article-title><source>J Fam Plann ReprodHealth Care</source><year>2005</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>40</fpage><lpage>43</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1783/0000000052972825</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B35"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Anyinam</surname><given-names>CA</given-names></name></person-group><source>Persistence with change: A rural-urban study of ethno &#x02013; cultural medical practices in contemporary Ghana [Unpublished PhD]</source><year>1987</year><publisher-name>Kingston: Queen's University</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B36"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bonsi</surname><given-names>SK</given-names></name></person-group><source>Traditional Medical Practice in Modern Ghana [Unpublished PhD]</source><year>1973</year><publisher-name>Missouri: University of Missouri-Columbia</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B37"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Anyinam</surname><given-names>CA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Availability, accessibility, acceptability, and adaptability: Four attributes of African ethno-medicine</article-title><source>Soc Sci Med</source><year>1987</year><volume>25</volume><fpage>803</fpage><lpage>811</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3317889</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/0277-9536(87)90038-4</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B38"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Cornwell</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><source>Hard earned lives: Accounts of health &#x00026; illness from East London</source><year>1984</year><publisher-name>London, Tavistock</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B39"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Foster</surname><given-names>GM</given-names></name><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>BG</given-names></name></person-group><source>Medical Anthropology</source><year>1978</year><publisher-name>New York, John Wiley</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B40"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fink</surname><given-names>HE</give